; Options for the Symphony/Dynphony GCC target.

; Symphony (the default) is the fixed 4-byte instruction encoding of this
; ISA. Dynphony is the variable-length encoding of the identical
; instruction set/semantics/ABI. GCC's instruction selection, RTL
; patterns, and calling convention are IDENTICAL for both -- only the
; final byte encoding differs, and that is handled entirely by the
; assembler+linker tool outside GCC (gcc_assembler.py), not by GCC
; itself. -mdynphony only changes the assembler-comment target marker
; GCC writes into the .s file header so the downstream assembler knows
; which encoding to use; it changes no codegen decision.

mdynphony
Target Var(symphony_dynphony) Init(0)
Generate assembly annotated for the Dynphony (variable-length) encoding instead of Symphony (fixed 4-byte) encoding.  Both share identical instruction selection; this only marks which downstream assembler encoding is intended.
